{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-91443",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We can solve this equation by isolating the $(x+3)^{6}$ term and then taking the $6$th root of each side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+3)^{6}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(x+3)^{6} & =512 \\\\ (x+3)^{6} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $6$th root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since the exponent $(6)$ is even, we consider both the positive and negative solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{6} & =64 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{(x+3)^{6}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt[6]{{2}^{6}} \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-1$ and $x=-5.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
